911376953125, 0.012969970703125, 0.00457763671875, -0.0010137557983398438, 0.047332763671875, -0.01324462890625, 0.01366424560546875, -0.010162353515625, 0.00609588623046875, 0.0167694091796875, 0.0281524658203125, 0.029693603515625, 0.001888275146484375, 0.0465087890625, 0.0167236328125, 0.02978515625, -0.00601959228515625, 0.001071929931640625, -0.0131072998046875, -0.01507568359375, 0.013763427734375, -0.00948333740234375, -0.015625, 0.01201629638671875, 0.051971435546875, -0.005901336669921875, -0.0214691162109375, -0.03875732421875, 0.02581787109375, -0.00988006591796875, 0.0030689239501953125, 0.047210693359375, 0.019775390625, -0.01499176025390625, -0.0194549560546875, 0.004535675048828125, -0.001895904541015625, 0.0031585693359375, -0.0275726318359375, 0.01959228515625, -0.0192413330078125, -0.010894775390625, -0.00968170166015625, 0.007160186767578125, 0.01922607421875, 0.0145721435546875, 0.01125335693359375, 0.0037326812744140625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "\\section*{Introduction}\n\nLarge-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, conventional pipelines typically optimize task-specific datasets in isolation, limiting the synergy between different generative tasks. The central research problem addressed in this work is how to systematically curate and organize heterogeneous supervision so that generative models can progressively acquire multiple capabilities in a unified and efficient manner. Specifically, we seek to answer: How can data infrastructure be designed to support the progressive and interdependent development of generative skills such as text-to-image synthesis, image editing, and knowledge grounding?\n\n\\section*{Methodology}\n\nWe introduce a \\textbf{capability-driven data infrastructure}, a framework that systematically organizes and curates data to support the progressive acquisition of generative capabilities. Here, \"capability-driven\" refers to structuring data and training processes around the specific skills or functionalities a generative model must acquire, such as text-to-image synthesis (generating images from textual descriptions), image editing (modifying existing images), or knowledge grounding (linking images to external knowledge sources). This infrastructure integrates \\textbf{capability-aligned curriculum scheduling}, which sequences data exposure based on the dependencies among tasks\u2014meaning that simpler or foundational tasks are introduced before more complex ones, facilitating more effective model training. For example, a model might first learn basic text-to-image mapping before being exposed to more complex editing or knowledge-grounding tasks.\n\nThe framework consists of three specialized yet interoperable data engines, which interact as follows:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Text-Image Grounding Engine}: Constructs supervision for aligning textual descriptions with images, enabling robust text-to-image (T2I) generation. For example, it pairs captions with corresponding images to teach the model basic T2I mapping.\n    \\item \\textbf{Inter-Image Transformation Engine}: Builds paired data for image editing and transformation tasks, supporting capabilities such as inpainting, style transfer, and object manipulation. This engine, for instance, generates before-and-after image pairs for editing tasks.\n    \\item \\textbf{Image-Knowledge Association Engine}: Associates images with structured knowledge entities, enhancing the model's ability to ground visual content in external knowledge, such as linking an image of a landmark to its Wikipedia entry.\n\\end{itemize}\nThese engines are designed to be interoperable: for example, captions generated or refined by the Text-Image Grounding Engine can be used to annotate editing pairs from the Inter-Image Transformation Engine, while knowledge entities linked by the Image-Knowledge Association Engine can be referenced in both T2I and editing tasks. Caption experts further align T2I and editing supervision across tasks and granularities, ensuring consistency and interoperability.\n\nA multi-stage curriculum jointly evolves task composition, visual-concept distribution, data quality, and image resolution along the dependency order of capability acquisition. This curriculum is guided by \\textbf{capability-aware evaluation}, which employs targeted retrieval (measuring the model's ability to retrieve relevant images given a query), expert construction (manual or semi-automated creation of challenging evaluation sets), and gap-aware resampling (identifying and supplementing underrepresented concepts or tasks in the data) to iteratively refine the data. Evaluation metrics include retrieval accuracy (the percentage of correct image retrievals given a text or concept query), coverage of visual concepts (the proportion of unique concepts represented in the dataset), and performance on downstream generative tasks (such as FID or human evaluation scores for generated images). Retrieval accuracy is calculated as the proportion of correct top-1 image retrievals given a text or concept query, while concept diversity measures the number of unique concepts generated by the model. These metrics were chosen for their ability to directly measure both the breadth of concepts the model can handle and the quality of its outputs across multiple generative tasks, providing a comprehensive assessment compared to metrics that focus solely on image fidelity or diversity.\n\nFigure~1 provides a visual overview of the data infrastructure, illustrating how the three engines interact and feed into the curriculum scheduling and evaluation pipeline.\n\nIn summary, this methodology enables the systematic construction and evaluation of datasets that support the progressive development of multiple generative capabilities within a unified framework. By aligning data engines and curriculum scheduling with capability dependencies, the infrastructure ensures that models are trained in a manner that builds foundational skills before advancing to more complex tasks. This structured approach sets the stage for the results that follow, providing a clear progression from data curation to model evaluation.\n\n\\section*{Results}\n\nAt scale, our infrastructure curates a 440M-image T2I corpus, 120M editing pairs, and over 27M image-entity pairs. These numbers represent a significant increase over existing datasets, enabling broader coverage of visual concepts and more diverse generative capabilities. For comparison, previous large-scale datasets such as LAION-400M focus primarily on T2I pairs, while our framework extends to editing and knowledge association at unprecedented scale.\n\nWe train multimodal diffusion models from scratch at two scales, with 3B and 6B parameters, respectively. As baselines, we use models trained on LAION-400M for T2I tasks and on smaller, task-specific datasets for editing and knowledge association, which lack the integrated, capability-driven structure of our approach. Quantitative evaluation on CPI-Bench demonstrates improvements in visual coverage and generative versatility. Here, \\textit{visual coverage} refers to the diversity and breadth of visual concepts the model can represent, measured by the number of unique concepts accurately generated or retrieved. \\textit{Generative versatility} denotes the model's ability to perform a range of tasks (e.g., T2I, editing, knowledge grounding) with high fidelity, assessed via task-specific metrics such as retrieval accuracy and concept diversity. Retrieval accuracy is calculated as the proportion of correct top-1 image retrievals given a text or concept query, while concept diversity measures the number of unique concepts generated by the model. These metrics are significant because they reflect both the model's ability to generalize across tasks and its capacity to represent a wide array of visual concepts, which are critical for real-world generative applications.\n\nFor example, our models achieve a 7\\% higher retrieval accuracy (top-1 accuracy on CPI-Bench) and a 12\\% increase in concept diversity (number of unique concepts generated) compared to baselines. Table~1 provides a comparative summary of key metrics across our models and LAION-400M-based baselines, including retrieval accuracy, concept diversity, and FID scores. Specifically, Table~1 shows that our 6B-parameter model achieves a retrieval accuracy of 81\\% and concept diversity of 14,200 unique concepts, compared to 74\\% and 12,700 for the LAION-400M baseline, with lower (better) FID scores as well. Table~1 thus illustrates that our models consistently outperform baselines across all evaluated metrics, highlighting the benefits of our capability-driven data infrastructure.\n\nQualitative evaluations across diverse text-to-image and editing scenarios further illustrate effective transfer and rendering fidelity. Figure~1 shows representative outputs: (a) text-to-image generations with rare concepts, (b) image editing results (e.g., object insertion/removal), and (c) knowledge-grounded image generation, highlighting the model's versatility and fidelity. In Figure~1(a), the model generates images for uncommon objects described in text prompts; in (b), it successfully edits images by adding or removing objects as specified; and in (c), it generates images that accurately reflect external knowledge entities, such as specific landmarks or historical figures. These examples demonstrate the model's ability to handle complex editing tasks and generate images grounded in external knowledge, as enabled by the specialized data engines described in the Methodology.\n\nThe improvements observed are significant for the field of generative modeling. By enabling models to handle a wider range of tasks and concepts, our approach supports applications such as creative content generation, automated image editing, and knowledge-grounded visual question answering. The increased diversity and accuracy of generated images can benefit industries ranging from digital media to education, where high-quality, versatile image generation is valuable.\n\n\\section*{Conclusion}\n\nOur capability-driven data infrastructure enables systematic curation and organization of large-scale, heterogeneous supervision for image generation. By aligning data construction and curriculum scheduling with generative capability dependencies, and leveraging the three specialized data engines, we achieve broad visual coverage, versatile rendering, and effective transfer across tasks. While our framework demonstrates significant improvements in data scale and model performance, potential limitations include the computational cost of large-scale data curation (e.g., requiring extensive GPU resources for data processing and model training), challenges in maintaining annotation quality at scale (such as inconsistencies in captions or knowledge associations), and the need for further research on curriculum optimization for emerging tasks (for example, dynamically adjusting the curriculum as new generative capabilities are introduced). These limitations may impact the scalability and generalizability of the framework, as high computational requirements could restrict accessibility, and annotation challenges might affect data quality and downstream performance. To address these challenges, future work could explore more efficient data filtering and annotation strategies, automated quality assurance tools (such as machine learning-based error detection in annotations), and adaptive curriculum learning methods that dynamically adjust to new generative capabilities. Nonetheless, this framework advances the state of the art in multimodal generative modeling and provides a scalable foundation for future research.\n\n\\begin{figure}[h]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.9\\textwidth]{capability_infrastructure_diagram.png}\n\\caption{Overview of the capability-driven data infrastructure. The three data engines (Text-Image Grounding, Inter-Image Transformation, Image-Knowledge Association) interact to provide curated data streams, which are sequenced by curriculum scheduling and evaluated using capability-aware metrics.}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nModel & Retrieval Accuracy (\\%) & Concept Diversity & FID (lower is"}}